.TH BX 1 "2026-10-20" bx "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bx \- Bash eXecutor, a configurable build automation and recipe invocation tool
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBbx\fR [\fB\-f\fR \fIbashfile\fR] [options] [\fB\-\-\fR] [recipe ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fIbx\fR utility is a build automation and recipe invocation tool that runs entirely in \fBBash 3.2+\fR.
It reads a configuration file called a \fIBashfile\fR, treats Bash functions declared in it as \fIrecipes\fR, and invokes them by name:
.PP
.RS
\fBbx\fR \fIrecipe\fR
.RE
.PP
Recipes are Bash functions declared in the \fIBashfile\fR; private functions prefixed by \fB_\fR, by convention, are not treated as recipes.
The names \fBbx\fR and \fBbx::*\fR are also reserved by \fIbx\fR and cannot be used as recipes either.
.PP
A recipe declared in the file \fIBashfile\fR as follows:
.PP
.RS
.nf
lint() {
  shellcheck "src/*.sh"
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
is invoked with
.PP
.RS
\fBbx\fR \fIlint\fR
.RE
.PP
When no recipe is given, \fIbx\fR invokes the default recipe, marked with the \fB@default\fR annotation (see ANNOTATIONS below).
If no default recipe is declared, \fIbx\fR fails with an error.
.PP
The \fIBashfile\fR may be specified explicitly with the \fB\-f\fR option.
Otherwise, \fIbx\fR looks for the nearest \fIBashfile\fR by walking up the directory tree from the current working directory (see FILES below).
This allows recipes to be invoked from any subdirectory of a project.
However, the user is responsible for ensuring the \fIBashfile\fR works regardless of the directory \fIbx\fR is invoked from.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP 0.5i
\fB\-f\fR \fIfile\fR, \fB\-\-file\fR=\fIfile\fR, \fB\-\-bashfile\fR=\fIfile\fR
Read \fIfile\fR as a bashfile instead of searching for the default \fIBashfile\fR.
Only one bashfile may be specified.
.TP 0.5i
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Display the usage line, the list of options, and the available recipes, together with any recipe descriptions declared with the \fB@help\fR annotation.
.TP 0.5i
\fB\-l\fR, \fB\-\-list\fR
Display the available recipes, together with any recipe descriptions declared with the \fB@help\fR annotation.
.TP 0.5i
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Do not display the invoked recipe traces, nor the xtrace output.
This is useful when the recipes themselves print the desired output.
.TP 0.5i
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Display the version of \fIbx\fR and exit without requiring a \fIBashfile\fR.
.TP 0.5i
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Skip all confirmation prompts and invoke every recipe automatically (see \fB@confirm\fR under ANNOTATIONS below).
.TP 0.5i
\fB\-\-\fR
Treat all remaining arguments as recipes, even if they begin with a hyphen.
.SH RECIPES
\fIbx\fR may invoke several recipes in a single execution, in the order given:
.PP
.RS
\fBbx\fR \fIformat\fR \fIlint\fR
.RE
.PP
Arguments may be passed to a recipe by quoting the recipe name together with its arguments.
They are made available to the recipe as \fI$1\fR, \fI$2\fR, etc:
.PP
.RS
\fBbx\fR \fI'lint --some-lint-option'\fR
.RE
.PP
A recipe that has already been invoked may legitimately be invoked again with different arguments.
However, when a recipe is already in the middle of being invoked, directly or indirectly, and is invoked again on the same invocation path, the re\-invocation is skipped with a warning.
This prevents circular invocation.
.SS "Nested invocation"
A recipe may invoke other recipes at runtime, without spawning a separate \fBbx\fR process, using the \fBbx::invoke\fR helper:
.PP
.RS
.nf
composite-recipe() {
  # Pre-processing...

  bx::invoke recipe-1
  bx::invoke recipe-2

  # Post-processing...
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
The \fBbx::invoke\fR helper accepts the same syntax as the command line: several recipes may be passed, and arguments are forwarded to the invoked recipe.
.PP
Passing \fB\-y\fR (or \fB\-\-yes\fR) to \fBbx::invoke\fR temporarily auto\-confirms all nested recipe invocations that require confirmation (see ANNOTATIONS below):
.PP
.RS
.nf
daredevil-recipe() {
  # Pre-processing...

  bx::invoke --yes destructive-recipe

  # Post-processing...
}

destructive-recipe() {
  @confirm

  # Do something destructive...
}
.fi
.RE
.SS "Scope boundaries"
Recipes declared as regular functions (syntax \fIrecipe_name() { ... }\fR) share the same scope:
if they modify global variables, the changes will be visible from other recipes.
.PP
Recipes declared as a subprocess (syntax \fIrecipe_name() ( ... )\fR) operate in a fork.
Nested invocations inside a subprocess recipe see its global changes; later top-level invocations do not.
.SS "Shell options isolation"
Shell options, such as \fBset\fR \fI\-x\fR for tracing, are isolated locally.
Option changes made inside a recipe do not leak into other recipes, nor into \fIbx\fR itself.
.PP
To preserve quiet mode, \fIbx\fR shadows the \fBset\fR builtin with a function of its own.
When \fB\-q\fR (or \fB\-\-quiet\fR) is in effect, an \fIxtrace\fR enabled by a recipe's \fBset\fR \fI\-x\fR is turned back off, without affecting the rest of the recipe.
.SH TRACES
Unless \fB\-q\fR (or \fB\-\-quiet\fR) is given, \fIbx\fR prints a trace line to standard error before and after each invoked recipe.
The trace is prefixed with a character consistent with \fBPS4\fR (default \fB+\fR), and repeated once per nesting level:
.PP
.RS
.nf
+ # top-level-recipe {
++ # nested-recipe {
++ # }
+ # }
.fi
.RE
.SH ANNOTATIONS
Annotations are directives placed inside a recipe body.
They are read by \fIbx\fR when loading the \fIBashfile\fR and instruct it on how to treat the recipe.
.PP
All annotations must appear at the top of the recipe body, before any executable instruction.
When the \fIBashfile\fR is loaded, \fIbx\fR validates this placement.
The validation applies to the whole \fIBashfile\fR: if any recipe contains a misplaced annotation, \fIbx\fR fails, even when a different recipe is invoked.
.TP 0.5i
\fB@help\fR \fIdescription ...\fR
Associates a short description with the recipe.
The description is displayed by \fBbx\fR \fB\-l\fR, and may span multiple lines by passing several arguments:
.PP
.RS
.nf
recipe() {
  @help "Line 1" \\
    "Line 2"
}
.fi
.RE
.TP 0.5i
\fB@default\fR
Marks the recipe as the default recipe, invoked when \fBbx\fR is executed without an explicit recipe:
.PP
.RS
.nf
recipe() {
  @default
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
At most one recipe may be marked as the default.
A default recipe must not be a private function (prefixed by \fB_\fR by convention).
.TP 0.5i
\fB@confirm\fR
Marks the recipe as requiring interactive confirmation before execution.
When the recipe is about to be invoked, \fIbx\fR prompts the user with a \fB[y/N]\fR question and waits for a single\-character reply.
Pressing \fBy\fR confirms the invocation; any other input (including an empty reply) cancels it and aborts the \fIbx\fR execution.
.PP
.RS
.nf
destructive-recipe() {
  @confirm

  rm -rf ./build
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
The prompt is shown for every matching recipe in a chain, including recipes invoked through nested invocation via \fBbx::invoke\fR.
Passing \fB\-y\fR (or \fB\-\-yes\fR) on the command line bypasses all confirmation prompts so that every recipe runs automatically.
.SH FILES
.TP 0.5i
\fIBashfile\fR
The default configuration file read by \fIbx\fR.
When the \fB\-f\fR option is not given, \fIbx\fR searches for the nearest \fIBashfile\fR starting from the current working directory and walking up its parents, until one is found or the root directory is reached.
If none is found, \fIbx\fR fails, unless \fB\-h\fR or \fB\-v\fR was requested.
.SH UPDATE
Update an existing \fIbx\fR installation with:
.PP
.RS
\fBbx\-update\fR
.RE
.SH UNINSTALL
Uninstall an existing \fIbx\fR installation with:
.PP
.RS
\fBbx\-uninstall\fR
.RE
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
\fIbx\fR exits with a status of zero if all invoked recipes completed successfully.
A nonzero status is returned if any recipe, or \fIbx\fR itself, encountered an error.
Error messages are printed to standard error with the \fBbx:\fR prefix.
